      "name": null,
      "problem": "Given the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 & 2 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 3 & 1\\end{aligned}]$, find a basis of $\\text{Null}(A).$",
      "worked_solution": "First, we reduce the matrix $A$ to reduced row echelon form (RREF):\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 & 2 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 3 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :=-{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -2 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 3 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-1){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 5 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, equation $Ax=0$ is equivalent to the system\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}-2{x}_{3}+2{x}_{4}=0 \\\\ {x}_{2}+5{x}_{3}-{x}_{4}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe general solution is ${x}_{1}=2{x}_{3}-2{x}_{4}$ and ${x}_{2}=-5{x}_{3}+{x}_{4},$ where ${x}_{3}$ and ${x}_{4}$ are free variables. Therefore, if $x\\in \\text{Null}(A),$ then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{4}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{3}-2{x}_{4} \\\\ -5{x}_{3}+{x}_{4} \\\\ {x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{4}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{3} \\\\ -5{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}-2{x}_{4} \\\\ {x}_{4} \\\\ 0 \\\\ {x}_{4}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={x}_{3}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -5 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{4}[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{3},{x}_{4}\\in R.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis implies that \n\n$$\nB=\\{[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -5 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\}\n$$\n\nis a basis of $\\text{Null}(A)$ since it is a linearly independent set and its vectors generate $\\text{Null}(A).$",
